The approved provider must engage an independent consultant to assist in lifting the quality of the service and its compliance with the Children (Education and Care Services) National Law (NSW) and Education and Care Services National Regulations until 1 December 2024.  The consultant must specialise in early childhood education and have the ability to support improvements to practices at the service with a focus on the following areas:
a)	Compliance with Section 167 - Offence relating to protection of children from harm and hazards;
b)	Compliance with Regulation 155 - Interactions with children;
c)	Compliance with Section 165 of the National Law - Offence to inadequately supervise children;
d)	Compliance with Regulation 170 - to ensure Policies and procedures required under Regulation 168 are followed;
e)	Compliance with Regulation 103 - Premises, furniture and equipment to be safe, clean and in good repair;
f)	Notification requirements to the Regulatory Authority including compliance with Section 173 and 174 of the National Law;
g)	Compliance with Section 175 of the National Law – requirement to keep enrolment, working directly with children and responsible person and other 
        records, including prescribed records under Regulation 177(2), and
h)	Compliance with Regulation 97 - Emergency and evacuation procedures The approved provider must, within 15 business days from the date of this letter, submit evidence of the engagement of the consultant including:
a)	The nature of services to be provided by the consultant; and,
b)	The time period of the engagement. The approved provider must submit a Quality Improvement Plan or Self- Assessment for the service to the Regulatory Authority by 1 January 2024, and every 3 months thereafter to 1 January 2025. The Quality Improvement Plan must include an assessment by the approved provider of the quality of the practices of the service against the National Quality Standard. It must also include any areas identified by the approved provider that require improvement, and a statement of the philosophy of the service. This plan must demonstrate a proactive commitment to continuous improvement The approved provider must ensure that the CCTV and monitors installed at the service are maintained and remain operational. Evidence that all CCTV monitors are operating is required to be provided to the Department by 1 January 2024.

The approved provider must ensure that all staff have read and are familiar with the services multi-level policy and procedures from transporting children between spaces. The approved provider must ensure that at all times children are being educated and cared for, that one additional educator above the minimum prescribed in regulation 123(1) of the Education and Care Services National Regulations 2011 is available to ensure adequate supervision of children when accessing hygiene facilities from the outdoor play area on the lower level. The Approved Provider is required to ensure objects are not placed in a manner that may allow a child to climb over barriers or fences; and, all play equipment in the outdoor play spaces are maintained and placed in a way that meets the requirements of the Australian Playground Standards (Standard AS 4685) with particular consideration given to falling space, impact areas and free space. The Approved Provider must engage an appropriate expert such as a fire safety advisor or fire safety engineer to observe and prepare a report on the emergency and evacuation procedure rehearsal of the service. This must occur at the first rehearsal of the emergency and evacuation procedure. The report prepared must identify:
h) the service;
i) the date and time of the rehearsal;
j) the total time taken to evacuate the building;
k) the location to which building occupants were evacuated to;
l) the number of children and educators in attendance on the day;
m) the profile of children (ages, any disabilities) with respect to the children’s rooms operating at the time of the rehearsal; and
n) any proposed actions the service must take to improve evacuation procedures to ensure the safety of all children attending the service.
The approved provider must submit a copy of any report produced in relation this condition to the Regional Office of the Regulatory Authority in which the service is located within seven (7) days of receipt.

The Approved Provider is required to ensure objects are not placed in a manner that may allow a child to climb over barriers or fences; and all play equipment in the outdoor play spaces are maintained and placed in a way that meets the requirements of the Australian Playground Standards (Standard AS 4685) with particular consideration given to falling space, impact areas and free space. The Approved Provider must ensure that they incorporate and maintain natural elements and features within outdoor spaces. The Approved Provider must ensure all educators are aware of and follow the outdoor risk assessment and procedures to provide enhanced supervision in the outdoor space and minimise the risk of serious injuries to children. The Approved Provider must engage an appropriate expert such as a fire safety advisor or fire safety engineer to observe and prepare a report on the emergency and evacuation procedure rehearsal of the service. This must occur at the first rehearsal of the emergency and evacuation procedure. The report prepared must identify: 

a) the service; 

b) the date and time of the rehearsal; 

c) the total time taken to evacuate the building; 

d) the location to which building occupants were evacuated to; 

e) the number of children and educators in attendance on the day; 

f) the profile of children (ages, any disabilities) with respect to the children’s rooms operating at the time of the rehearsal; and 

g) any proposed actions the service any proposed actions the service must take to improve evacuation procedures to ensure the safety of all children attending the service. 

The approved provider must submit a copy of any report produced in relation this condition to the Regional Office of the Regulatory Authority in which the service is located within seven (7) days of receipt.

The maximum number of family day care educators that may be engaged or registered by the approved provider is 15. The approved provider must identify and maintain an up to date record of this service’s family day care educators operating in a family day care residence or approved family day care venue in Victoria who may be at risk of bushfire (bushfire-at-risk educators); 

For any bushfire-at-risk educators, the approved provider must ensure that: 

bushfire-at-risk educators do not operate on any day declared by the Victorian Emergency Management Commissioner to be a day of ‘Code Red’ Fire Danger Rating that would apply to the location in which that family day care residence or approve family day care venue is located; and 

the service’s emergency and evacuation policies and procedures required under regulation 168 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations include a current Emergency Management Plan (EMP) for managing the service’s bush-fire related risks; 

the Emergency Management Plan (EMP) is kept up to date, reflects the family day care educator’s operating context and includes: 

procedures for monitoring and managing bushfire risks and events; 

procedures for notifying families of any closure 

the Emergency Management Plan (EMP) is reviewed and updated as necessary at least once each calendar year. The approved provider must notify the Regulatory Authority within 5 days of the commencement of the operation of the family day care service. The approved provider within 5 days of the commencement of the operation of the family day care service must submit to the Regulatory Authority: 

a copy of the register of family day care educators, co-ordinators and assistants that complies with section 269 of the Education and Care Services National Law Act 2010 and regulation 153 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations 2011. 

a copy of the record of staff engaged or employed by the family day care service that complies with regulation 154 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations 2011.

