It is a condition of this service approval that the Approved Provider:
•	Must employ or engage at least 1 full time equivalent family day co-ordinator for every 15 family day care educators engaged by or registered with the family day care service; It is a condition of this service approval that the Approved Provider:
•	Must only engage or register educators with the family day care service who provide education and care in Brisbane City Council, Ipswich City Council and Logan City Council local government areas. It is a condition of this service approval that the Approved Provider:  Must not engage or register more than 50 family day care educators with the service;

This service is approved to provide education and care to children from birth to over preschool age.  The mirror installed at the service is constantly maintained to ensure the facilitation of supervision of children at all times they are being educated and cared for at the service, having regard to the need to maintain the rights and dignity of the children as required under Regulation 115 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations. The service is not approved to provide education and care to school children. 
i.	As part of the requirement to conduct three (3) monthly emergency and evacuation procedure rehearsals in accordance with regulation 97(3) of the Education and Care Services National Regulations, the Approved Provider must engage an appropriate expert, such as a person/entity holding a Queensland Building and Construction Commission licence in Emergency Procedures, a fire safety advisor or a fire safety engineer, to observe and report on one full rehearsal by the end of March each year;
ii.	A copy of each annual evacuation outcome report, including any recommendations for actioning is to be supplied to the Regional Office of the Regulatory Authority in which the service is located within 7 days upon receipt of the report by the Approved Provider. The Approved Provider must ensure that a ratio across the service of 1 staff member to 2 non-ambulant infants is maintained at all times education and care is provided to non-ambulant infants to assist in the safe evacuation of these children from the service during regular rehearsals and any actual emergency. Staff who are responsible for evacuating non-ambulant infants are not to be responsible for evacuating any other children, other than children who are 3-5 years old. Staff who are responsible for evacuating children aged 2-3 years are not to be responsible for evacuating any other children. The Approved Provider must not educate and care for more than 16 non-ambulant infants at any one time.

The CCTV and monitors installed at the service are constantly maintained and operational to ensure the facilitation of supervision of children at all times they are being educated and cared for at the service, having regard to the need to maintain the rights and dignity of the children as required under regulation 115 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations. The service is approved to provide education and care to children under two years of age on the ground floor of the service only At all times education and care is being provided to children in the roof top outdoor play area, at least two (2) educators must be working directly with the children and the service must also ensure it continues to comply with the educator to child ratio as outlined in the National Regulations on this level. As part of the requirement to conduct three (3) monthly emergency and evacuation procedures rehearsals in accordance with regulation 97(3) of the Education and Care Services National Regulations, the Approved Provider must, engage an appropriate expert such as a person/entity holding a QBCC license in emergency procedures, a fire safety advisor or fire safety engineer to observe and report on one rehearsal at least annually by the end of April every year. The Approved Provider must ensure that no outdoor play equipment is placed within one metre of the perimeter fencing in the roof top outdoor play area. A copy of the annual evacuation outcome report is to be supplied to the Regional Office of the Regulatory Authority in which the service is located within seven (7) days upon receipt of the report by the Approved Provider together with any proposed actions to improve evacuation procedures. CCTV cameras and monitors are to be installed, constantly maintained and operational in the service lifts and used to facilitate supervision.  A staff member must be engaged as a Transition Attendant between the hours of 7:30am - 5:30pm daily to ensure that children are safely and freely able to transition between the classrooms and the rooftop playground. The Transition Attendant:
▪ cannot be counted towards the prescribed educator to child ratio requirements;
▪ must be equipped with a ‘walkie talkie’ to enable communication with staff inside the service;
▪ must complete a sweep of the area that the children transitioned away from to ensure that no child is left behind The service must conduct attendance checks following transitions between the rooftop playground and classrooms From 1 March 2023, CCTV cameras with Artificial Intelligence (AI) capability must be installed and operational on the rooftop playground at all times having regard to the need to maintain the rights and dignity of children as required under regulation 115 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations. The AI technology must use an application to notify staff when a person is detected on the rooftop during periods that the rooftop playground is not in use The Transition Attendant must be equipped with a dedicated device with the AI CCTV application installed and with notifications enabled, once this technology is operational. From 1 April 2023, the service must conduct unannounced compliance audits of the Multi-Level Services Transition Procedure on a quarterly basis. Records of these audits must be kept and made available for inspection by the Queensland Regulatory Authority. The service is approved to provide education and care to children from birth up to and including over preschool age This service is not approved to provide education and care to school children

The service is approved to provide education and care to children from birth up to and including over preschool age.
 The service is not approved to provide education and care to school children The Approved Provider for the service must not provide education and care to  more than sixteen (16) non-ambulant infants at any one time. The mirrors, CCTV and monitors installed at the service are constantly maintained and operational to ensure the facilitation of supervision of children at all times they are being educated and cared for at the service, having regard to the need to maintain the rights and dignity of the children as required under regulation 115 of the Education and Care Services National Regulations (National Regulations). a) The service must have at least 7 square metres of combined actual outdoor and enclosed outdoor space for each child being educated and cared for;
 b) To ensure ongoing compliance with natural environment requirements under regulation 113 of the National Regulations, the serice is required to maintain or increase the current number of healthy live plants in a variety of species and sizes in the enclosed outdoor space of the premises.

The Approved Provider is required to ensure objects are not placed in a manner that may allow a child to climb over barriers or fences; and, all play equipment in the outdoor play spaces are maintained and placed in a way that meets the requirements of the Australian Playground Standards (Standard AS 4685:2014) with particular consideration given to falling space, impact areas and free space. The Approved Provider must ensure that they incorporate and maintain natural elements and features within outdoor play spaces. The Approved Provider must ensure that a minimum of two educators are working directly with children being educated and cared for on level 1. An additional educator above the prescribed educator to child ratios is required at all times when transporting children between levels and to the rooftop outdoor play space. The service must develop and maintain a policy in relation to children’s access to outdoor space (outdoor space policy). The service’s outdoor space policy must provide for all children being educated and cared for by the service to have regular access to the roof top play space. The Approved Provider must take reasonable steps to ensure that the nominated supervisor and staff members of, and volunteers at, the service follow the services outdoor play policy relating to access to outdoor space. The Approved Provider must engage an appropriate expert such as fire safety advisor or fire safety engineer to observe and prepare a report on the emergency and evacuation procedure rehearsal of the service once it has transitioned. This must occur at the first rehearsal of the emergency and evacuation procedure after the transition. The report prepared must identify:
o		the service;
o		the date and time of the rehearsal;
o		the total time taken to evacuate the building;
o		the location to which building occupants were evacuated to;
o		the number of children and educators in attendance on the day;
o		the profile of children (ages, any disabilities) with respect to the children’s rooms operating at the time of the rehearsal; and
o		any proposed actions the service must take to improve evacuation procedures to ensure the safety of all children attending the service.

The Approved Provider must submit a copy of any report produced in relation this condition to the Regional Office of the Regulatory Authority in which the service is located within seven (7) days of receipt.

 
